# VRAM preset for a 24GB card (e.g. RTX 3090/4090, RX 7900 XTX).
#
# CONFIDENCE: best-guess extrapolation, NOT measured on real 24GB hardware -
# see presets/16gb.env's header for the full derivation method (same model,
# just scaled to a 24GB card's ~25.75GB real capacity with the same 15%
# safety margin). The 12GB->16GB step is already a stretch from one card's
# data; 12GB->24GB is a 2x extrapolation on top of a measured relationship
# that was already shown to be nonlinear (150->220 frames cost 4x more
# VRAM/frame than 110->150 did) - treat CHUNK_SIZE below as considerably
# less trustworthy than the 16GB preset's, not just proportionally scaled.
#
# TILE_NUM: DO NOT just lower this for more VRAM headroom on AMD/ROCm
# hardware - see presets/16gb.env's TILE_NUM note for the full explanation.
# Measured on the 12GB dev card: tile_num=2 needs manual chunked-attention
# fallback (chunked_attention.py) to avoid OOM at all on ROCm (no working
# flash/efficient kernel here), and that fallback runs ~9-11x slower per
# chunk than tile_num=4, with real tail-latency spikes (one tile hit 285s
# for a single step). A bigger card doesn't fix this - it's a compute
# scaling problem (attention cost ~ tile_area^2 without a real efficient
# kernel), not a VRAM one. Leave TILE_NUM=4 on AMD/ROCm regardless of card
# size, and spend the extra 24GB of headroom on CHUNK_SIZE instead.
#
# On NVIDIA/CUDA: flash-attention works there, so chunked_attention.py's
# slow fallback never triggers (it always tries the real kernel first) and
# lowering TILE_NUM should be a genuine speedup - try TILE_NUM=2 or even 1
# and compare wall-clock yourself; matches what NVIDIA users report in
# upstream issues (tile_num=2 fine on 24GB), but we have no NVIDIA hardware
# to have actually verified that this session.
#
# DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE is a separate, still-unverified guess.
#
# STRONGLY RECOMMENDED on this preset specifically: smoke-test before
# committing to a long run - PROCESS_LENGTH=$CHUNK_SIZE for a single-outer-
# chunk stage 1 run while watching peak VRAM, same as the 16GB preset
# suggests. If it OOMs, CHUNK_SIZE is the first thing to back off - the
# script prints exactly how many bytes it tried to allocate on an OOM crash,
# which tells you how far over you were.
#
# Usage:
#   source presets/24gb.env && ./run_stereo.sh input.mp4

# ---- stage 1 (depth splatting) ----
export CHUNK_SIZE=420       # est. peak ~21.9GB with CPU_OFFLOAD=None (85% of ~25.8GB) -
                             # low-confidence extrapolation, see header
export WINDOW_SIZE=70       # unchanged - validated for the cross-chunk consistency fix
export WINDOW_OVERLAP=25
export CPU_OFFLOAD=None

# ---- stage 2 (inpainting) ----
export TILE_NUM=4           # see note above - do NOT lower this on AMD/ROCm hardware
export DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE=4  # unverified guess
export DECODE_LATENTS_CHUNK_SIZE=4

# ---- resumability (leave this on - it's the safety net for the above being wrong) ----
export RESUME=True
export MAX_RETRIES=3
